The gap between the two district averages changed over time. In 2025, the average completed sale price was £585,850 in Oxford and £595,244 in Cambridge, a difference of £9,395.

## Cumulative nominal price growth

![Cumulative house price growth in Oxford and Cambridge](/images/oxford-vs-cambridge-house-prices-cumulative-growth.svg)

Measured from their respective 2000 averages, Oxford increased by **248.8%** and Cambridge by **290.0%** through 2025. This measures changes in the arithmetic mean of recorded transactions, not the return on a particular property.

## Annual price movements

![Year-on-year average price changes in Oxford and Cambridge](/images/oxford-vs-cambridge-house-prices-annual-change.svg)

Annual averages can be volatile, especially when the number or type of properties sold changes. The chart starts annual percentage changes in 2001; the 2000 value is a baseline and should not be interpreted as a measured annual change.

## Difference in average sale prices

![Average price gap: Oxford minus Cambridge between Oxford and Cambridge](/images/oxford-vs-cambridge-house-prices-price-gap.svg)

A positive value means Oxford's average sale price was higher in that year; a negative value means Cambridge's was higher. Price levels and percentage growth answer different questions, so both matter when comparing markets.

